---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { url } from '../lib/url';
---

<BaseLayout title="For grown-ups" description="How to help your child with Python at home, even if you have never coded.">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-text">
      <span class="eyebrow">Start here · for parents and carers</span>
      <h1>You don't need to know Python</h1>
      <p class="summary">
        Your job is to be a curious partner, not a teacher. Fifteen minutes, a few questions, and lots of
        encouragement.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="lesson-body">
    <h2>Your three jobs</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Read aloud.</strong> Read "The big idea" together. The comparisons (a waiter, a labelled box) are there for you as much as for them.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask "what will happen?"</strong> Before every press of Run, ask for a prediction, then ask "why?". Getting a prediction wrong is useful: it shows exactly what to look at next.</li>
      <li><strong>Check answers.</strong> The "Quiz me" questions have hidden answers. Read the question, let them answer, then press Reveal.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What the page does for you</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Run</strong> runs their program. If the program asks a question, a box appears to type the answer.</li>
      <li><strong>Check my code</strong> tests the program exactly as CodeBash does at school. Every space, capital letter and full stop has to match. Dots (·) show where the spaces are.</li>
      <li>If something is wrong, the page explains what to look at. Red error messages link to the <a href={url('/error-decoder/')}>error decoder</a>, which explains each one in plain English.</li>
      <li>The <a href={url('/key-words/')}>key words</a> page explains every word they meet.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Good things to say</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>"What do you think will happen? Why?"</li>
      <li>"Read me the last line of the error. What word does it start with?"</li>
      <li>"Which line do you think is the problem?"</li>
      <li>"You've fixed that one. Can you explain what was wrong?"</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Try not to type for them. Pointing at the screen and asking a question works better.</p>

    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>
      Python runs inside your web browser. Nothing your child types is sent anywhere: there are no accounts, no
      cookies and no tracking. Their ratings and saved code are stored on this computer only. The first time they
      press Run, the browser downloads Python (about 10 MB), so the first run can take a few seconds.
    </p>

    <h2>If you get stuck</h2>
    <p>
      That's normal. Note down which lesson and which task, and your child can bring it to their next Computer
      Science lesson.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
